  "story": {
    "title": "Their loss changed Appalachian forests for ever. Now the American chestnut is back",
    "summary": "The American chestnut once dominated Appalachian forests but was wiped out as a living canopy tree by a parasitic blight, leaving it functionally extinct for nearly a century. New breeding and genetic techniques are now producing young trees that resist the blight, and the species is returning.",
    "source": "The Guardian",
    "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/24/american-chestnut-tree-restored-science-persistence-aoe",
    "published": "2026-11-24T12:00:44.000Z",
    "theme": "forest restoration and trees"
  },
  "headlines": [
    "The Forest Remembers Its Giant",
    "Back From Functional Extinction",
    "A Century After the Blight"
  ],
  "sublines": [
    "Nearly a century after a parasitic blight erased them, American chestnuts are being regrown across Appalachia from resistant seedlings.",
    "New techniques are producing chestnut trees that can survive the fungus which once felled billions of stems across eastern forests.",
    "Once the backbone of Appalachian woodland, the chestnut vanished from the canopy; scientists and growers are now planting its successors."
